		Registers
		Program Counter
		Memory Mapping	
		Address Space
Thread Context Switch - 
	OS switches:
		Registers
		Program Counter
		Stack
	Memory remains shared

Is Context Switching Free?
	No.

Every switch consumes CPU time.

During switching:
	Saving State
	Loading State
	Scheduling

No useful application work is done.

This overhead is called:
	Context Switching Overhead
	
What triggers a Context Switch?
	Time quantum expires
	I/O operation request
	Process blocks
	Higher-priority process arrives
	Interrupt occurs
	
Does a Context Switch always involve a mode switch?
	Not necessarily.

	A context switch often requires kernel involvement.
	A mode switch is a transition between User Mode and Kernel Mode.
	
Context Switching is the mechanism by which the operating system saves the execution state of the currently running process or thread and restores the state of another process or thread, allowing the CPU to switch execution between them. The saved state includes information such as CPU registers, program counter, and stack pointer. Context switching enables multitasking but introduces overhead because the CPU spends time saving and restoring states instead of executing application code.

Why is thread context switching faster than process context switching?
	Because threads within the same process share the same address space and resources. The OS only needs to switch thread-specific information like registers and stack pointers, whereas process switching also requires changing memory mappings and other process-related resources.
	
