Types of Scheduling Algorithm

1. FCFS (First Come First Serve) -
	FCFS (First Come First Serve) is a non-preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm in which the process that arrives first in the ready queue is allocated the CPU first and runs until completion or until it blocks.
	
	Characteristics of FCFS
		Non-Preemptive
			Once a process gets the CPU:
			No other process can take the CPU until:
				P1 finishes, or
				P1 blocks for I/O.
				
	Advantages
		1. Simple to Implement
			Use a FIFO queue.
		2. Fair
			Processes are served in arrival order.
		3. Low Scheduling Overhead
			Very little decision-making required.
			
	Disadvantages
		1. Convoy Effect
			A long process can make many short processes wait.
		2. Poor Average Waiting Time
			Short processes can get stuck behind long ones.
		3. Not Suitable for Interactive Systems
			Users expect quick responses.
			
	FCFS (First Come First Serve) is a non-preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm where the process that arrives first is executed first. It uses a FIFO queue and is simple to implement. However, it can lead to poor average waiting times and the convoy effect, where short processes are forced to wait behind long-running processes.

2. SJF (Shortest Job First) -
	Shortest Job First (SJF) is a CPU scheduling algorithm that selects the process with the shortest burst time from the ready queue and executes it first. It minimizes average waiting time among all scheduling algorithms.
	
	Types of SJF
	1. Non-Preemptive SJF
		Once a process gets the CPU:
			it continues until completion.
			Even if a shorter process arrives later:
	2. Preemptive SJF (SRTF)
		Also called:
			Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)
		If a shorter process arrives, the currently running process can be interrupted.
		
	Advantages of SJF
		1. Lowest Average Waiting Time
			Its biggest advantage.
		2. Better Throughput
			More short jobs finish quickly.
		3. Efficient for Batch Systems
			Suitable when job lengths are known.
		
	Disadvantages of SJF
		1. Burst Time Must Be Known
			The OS usually doesn't know how long will this process run before execution.
			This is the biggest practical problem.
		2. Starvation
			The long process may wait indefinitely.
			
	Shortest Job First (SJF) is a CPU scheduling algorithm that selects the process with the smallest CPU burst time for execution. It minimizes average waiting time and improves throughput compared to FCFS. SJF can be non-preemptive or preemptive, where the preemptive version is called Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF). Its main disadvantages are the difficulty of predicting burst times and the possibility of starvation for long-running processes.

3. Round Robin - 
	Round Robin is a preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm in which each process is assigned a fixed time quantum. Processes are executed in a circular order, and if a process does not complete within its quantum, it is moved to the end of the ready queue.
	
	Advantages of Round Robin
		1. Fair Scheduling
			Every process gets CPU time.
			No process can monopolize the CPU.
		2. Good Response Time
			Suitable for:
				Interactive systems
				Time-sharing systems
		3. Prevents Starvation
			Unlike SJF:
				Long Process
			cannot wait forever.
			Every process eventually gets CPU time.
		4. Preemptive
			Supports multitasking effectively.
	Disadvantages of Round Robin
		1. Context Switching Overhead
			Frequent switching means Extra CPU work.
		2. Performance Depends on Quantum
			Bad quantum selection:
				Too Small → High overhead
				Too Large → FCFS behavior
		3. Average Waiting Time Can Be Higher
			Compared to SJF.
			SJF is more efficient but less fair.
			RR is more fair but may be less efficient.
			
	Round Robin is a preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm in which each process receives a fixed time quantum. If a process does not finish within its allocated quantum, it is preempted and moved to the end of the ready queue. Round Robin provides fairness, prevents starvation, and offers good response times, making it suitable for interactive and time-sharing systems. However, its performance depends heavily on the choice of time quantum, as very small quantums increase context-switching overhead while very large quantums make it behave similarly to FCFS.

4. Priority Scheduling -

	Priority Scheduling is a CPU scheduling algorithm in which each process is assigned a priority, and the CPU is allocated to the process with the highest priority among all ready processes.
	
	Types of Priority Scheduling
		1. Non-Preemptive Priority Scheduling
			Once a process gets the CPU it continues until completion.
		2. Preemptive Priority Scheduling
			If a higher-priority process arrives lower priority process stops and higher priority process takes cpu.
			
	How Priorities Are Assigned

	Priorities may be based on:
		Static Priority
			Assigned once and never changes.
		Dynamic Priority
			Priority changes over time.
	Advantages
		1. Important Tasks Execute First
		2. Flexible
			Can support:
				Real-time systems
				Embedded systems
				Critical applications
		3. Better Resource Allocation
			Critical work receives more CPU time.
	Disadvantages
		1. Starvation
			The biggest problem.
	Solution: Aging
		To prevent starvation:
			Gradually increase the priority of waiting processes over time.
			
	Priority Scheduling is a CPU scheduling algorithm where each process is assigned a priority, and the CPU is allocated to the highest-priority process. It can be implemented in both preemptive and non-preemptive forms. Its main advantage is that important processes are executed first, making it suitable for real-time and critical systems. However, it may lead to starvation of low-priority processes, which can be mitigated using aging.
	
