What is Mutex?
	Mutex stands for:
		Mutual Exclusion
	A mutex is a synchronization mechanism used to ensure that only one thread can access a critical section at a time.
	
Before entering the critical section:
	Acquire Lock
After leaving:
	Release Lock
	
Important Property
	Ownership
		A mutex has an owner.
	Thread 1 acquires mutex
		Only Thread 1 can release it.
	Thread 2 cannot unlock it

	This is one major difference from semaphores.
	
Can Mutex Cause Deadlock?
	Yes.
	
Advantages of Mutex
	1. Prevents Race Conditions
		Ensures safe access to shared data.
	2. Simple Concept
		Easy to understand and implement.
	3. Guarantees Mutual Exclusion
		Only one thread enters at a time.
		
Disadvantages of Mutex
	1. Deadlock Possibility
		Incorrect lock ordering can cause deadlock.
	2. Context Switching Overhead
		Threads may block and wake up frequently.
	3. Reduced Parallelism
		Only one thread accesses the critical section.
		
