Semaphore

A semaphore is a synchronization primitive that uses a counter to control access to shared resources. Threads perform wait (P) and signal (V) operations to decrement and increment the semaphore value.

Why Do We Need Semaphores?
	Suppose you have: Database Connection Pool
	with:
		5 connections
	and:
		20 threads
	
	trying to access it.

	Using a mutex:
		Only 1 thread allowed
	which wastes resources.
	
	Using a semaphore:
		Up to 5 threads allowed

	which matches the available resources.
	
A semaphore contains a counter. i.e - semaphore - 3

Operations:
	wait(p) - before entering apply wait() this decreases semaphore counter
	Signal (v) - after leaving apply signal() this increases semaphore counter
	
Types of Semaphores
	1. Binary Semaphore : 
		values  - 0 or 1 , only one thread enters others wait.
	2. Counting Semaphore
		Values: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, ...
		Allows multiple threads.
		
