Logical vs Physical Address - 

A logical address is the address generated by the CPU for a process. It is translated into a physical address by the Memory Management Unit (MMU).
A Physical Address is the actual address in the RAM where the data is stored.

The MMU is hardware that converts logical addresses into physical addresses.

Memory Mapping Example

	Suppose:

	Process A

	Logical Memory

		0
		100
		200
		300

	MMU translates:

		0   → 5000

		100 → 5100

		200 → 5200

		300 → 5300

	Actual RAM:

	RAM

		5000
		5100
		5200
		5300

	The process never sees these physical addresses.
	
Why Is This Useful?
	1. Memory Protection
		Each process has its own logical address space. Process A cannot access process B directly.
	2. Easy Memory Management
		The OS can move processes around in RAM without changing the program.
		The logical address stays the same.
		Only the mapping changes.
	3. Supports Virtual Memory
		Logical memory can be larger than physical RAM.
		
Logical vs Physical Address
Feature			Logical Address			Physical Address
Generated By		CPU				MMU after translation
Visible To		Process				Memory hardware
Also Called		Virtual Address			Real Address
Location		Virtual Memory			RAM
Can Be Changed		Yes (via mapping)		Actual location


A logical address, also called a virtual address, is the address generated by the CPU during program execution. A physical address is the actual location in RAM where data is stored. The Memory Management Unit (MMU) translates logical addresses into physical addresses. This abstraction provides memory protection, process isolation, and enables virtual memory.

